firefox: follow the add-on state of each profile

A profile that holds the QtCurve theme add-on in a not active
state now takes no sheet from a write pass. The pass removes the
owned sheet and the import line of such a profile, and it prints
one line with the restart truth. A profile with no QtCurve add-on
keeps the firefoxTheme key as its one switch, so the writer-only
flow holds.

The pass reads the add-on state from the extensions.json file of
the profile. The reader matches the add-on identifier, narrows on
the type theme, prefers a visible record on a duplicate, and takes
a missing active field as not active. An absent file means write;
a file that does not parse keeps the files of that profile in
place for one pass. The writer still writes no state file of
Firefox.

The watch mode now watches the extensions.json file of each
profile, so a theme switch inside Firefox reaches the writer
through the same debounce as every other change. --force keeps
the whole old behaviour for the build time runs and the checks.

assert-ff-state.sh proves the predicate over fixture state files,
and assert-ff-matrix.sh proves the cells of the key, force and
add-on matrix.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 11 of 14. The branch holds those fourteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

